---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../../components/SiteNav.astro';
---
<Base
  title="LLM vs. Logistic Regression Classifier Baseline · San Lee"
  description="A TF-IDF and logistic-regression baseline compared with an LLM on the same 54 hand-labeled rows, testing whether added model cost was justified."
  canonical="https://sanlee.me/projects/classifier-baseline.html"
  ogType="article"
>

  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* Baseline vs LLM fork (portfolio ADR-004). Page-scoped: why an LLM at
       all is the decision, not the bake-off percentages (the table below
       carries those). Max-width 260 keeps viewBox units 1:1 with CSS px on
       every gate width (same measured discipline as .two-fail / .judge-plate). */
    .bake-fork {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      max-width: 260px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 18px 0 6px;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-lane {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-title {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 500;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 8px;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* Gold target: ink solid — both arms face the same human set. Dual-
       encoded by word + stroke weight, not colour alone. */
    .bake-fork .bf-gold {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .bake-fork .bf-gold-text {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
    }
    .bake-fork marker path { fill: var(--muted); }
    .bake-fork-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      margin: 0 0 6px;
    }
  </style>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteNav current="work" />

  <style is:inline>
    /* ----- Page-specific styles only: the "run the loser" demo -------------
       Nothing here is reusable yet, so none of it goes in assets/style.css.
       Two constraints shaped it. First, the palette is two hues and the second
       one (--accent-warm) means "the record of something that was taken back"
       -- which is exactly what a baseline's wrong answer is, so the wrong class
       gets the warm token and nothing else invents a colour. Second, no text is
       ever dimmed with opacity (ADR-009): quieter text takes a quieter TOKEN,
       and opacity is reserved for the bar tracks, which carry no text. */

    .bdemo { margin: 26px 0 8px; }

    .bdemo-kicker {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 10px;
    }

    /* Chips. A 44px minimum on both axes is the mobile tap contract, and it is
       set here rather than left to the text's line-height, which would shrink
       with the font. */
    .bdemo-chips {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 8px;
      margin: 0 0 22px;
    }
    .bdemo-chip {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.85rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text);
      background: transparent;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
      padding: 10px 14px;
      min-height: 44px;
      cursor: pointer;
      transition: border-color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .bdemo-chip:hover { border-color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-chip.is-on {
      border-color: var(--accent);
      background: var(--accent-soft);
    }
    .bdemo-chip:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: 3px;
    }

    /* The free-text field, ruled rather than boxed, matching .glossary-filter. */
    .bdemo-label {
      display: block;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 6px;
    }
    .bdemo-input {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      /* border-box or the 1px border and the padding push this past the grid
         track and the page scrolls sideways on a phone. */
      box-sizing: border-box;
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: var(--text);
      background: transparent;
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: 10px 12px;
      min-height: 106px;
      resize: vertical;
      transition: border-color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .bdemo-input::placeholder { color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-input:hover { border-color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-input:focus-visible {
      outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
      outline-offset: 3px;
      border-color: var(--accent);
    }

    .bdemo-status {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      margin: 10px 0 24px;
      min-height: 1.5em;
    }

    /* Two panels, collapsing to one column with no breakpoint. */
    .bdemo-cols {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(255px, 1fr));
      gap: 28px 34px;
    }
    .bdemo-panel {
      border-top: 2px solid var(--rule-strong);
      padding-top: 14px;
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .bdemo-h {
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-weight: var(--display-weight);
      font-size: 1rem;
      letter-spacing: -0.01em;
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }

    .bdemo-axis { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .bdemo-axis-name {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 0 0 2px;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .bdemo-verdict {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 1.15rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text);
      margin: 0 0 10px;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .bdemo-verdict.is-wrong { color: var(--accent-warm); }
    .bdemo-verdict.is-right { color: var(--accent); }

    /* Bars. The track is the only place opacity appears, and it carries no
       text -- see the block header. */
    .bdemo-bar-row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 7.6em) 1fr 3.4em;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 8px;
      margin: 0 0 5px;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }
    .bdemo-bar-name {
      color: var(--muted);
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .bdemo-bar-name.is-top { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 500; }
    .bdemo-bar-name.is-wrong { color: var(--accent-warm); font-weight: 500; }
    .bdemo-bar-track {
      display: block;
      height: 9px;
      min-width: 0;
      background: var(--border);
    }
    .bdemo-bar-fill {
      display: block;
      height: 100%;
      background: var(--rule-strong);
    }
    .bdemo-bar-fill.is-top { background: var(--accent); }
    .bdemo-bar-fill.is-wrong { background: var(--accent-warm); }
    .bdemo-bar-val { color: var(--text-secondary); text-align: right; }

    .bdemo-nosignal {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--accent-warm);
      margin: 0;
    }

    .bdemo-missing,
    .bdemo-ref-note,
    .bdemo-ref-empty {
      font-size: 0.88rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--text-secondary);
      margin: 0 0 10px;
    }
    .bdemo-ref-prov,
    .bdemo-prov {
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      line-height: 1.55;
      font-weight: 500;
      color: var(--muted);
      margin: 14px 0 0;
    }
    .bdemo-prov { margin-top: 26px; }

    /* The recorded strip. Three columns, no <table>: the site's table rules are
       tuned for the wide comparison tables and would fight a three-cell strip
       inside an already-narrow panel. */
    .bdemo-ref-grid { margin: 0 0 14px; }
    .bdemo-ref-row {
      display: grid;
      /* The key column is the widest of the three because one of its two values
         is the 18-character `operational_domain`; at 1fr it broke mid-word. */
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.55fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 8px;
      padding: 7px 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.72rem;
      color: var(--text);
    }
    .bdemo-ref-row.is-head { color: var(--muted); border-bottom-color: var(--rule-strong); }
    .bdemo-ref-k { color: var(--muted); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .bdemo-ref-row.is-head .bdemo-ref-k { color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-ref-v { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .bdemo-ref-row.is-head .bdemo-ref-v { color: var(--muted); }
    .bdemo-ref-v.is-wrong { color: var(--accent-warm); }
  </style>

  <main class="wrap article-body" id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <a class="back" href="defense-news-classifier.html">&larr; Back to the classifier</a>
    <h1>Why an LLM at All</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">TF-IDF plus logistic regression, scored on the same gold as the LLM.</p>

    <p>
      The classifier writeup measures upgrades on top of a language model: a
      sharper prompt, retrieval, a bigger model for the hard rows. It does not
      answer whether the task needs an LLM.
    </p>
    <p>
      So I built the boring baseline: TF-IDF features into logistic regression,
      one classifier per axis, trained on 300 real snippets graded by the Opus
      judge (the scaled set, not v1&rsquo;s synthetic one) and scored once
      against the same 54 hand-labeled rows. Standard, not tuned.
    </p>

    <!-- Decision fork: same text, two arms, one human gold set. Percentages
         stay in the table below (ADR-004 Decision 5 floor — the figure carries
         topology, prose keeps the numbers). No oxide: this is a bake-off, not
         a reversal claim. ADR-010 N/A — no interactive nodes. -->
    <svg class="bake-fork" viewBox="0 0 260 148" role="img"
         aria-label="Decision fork for the classifier bake-off: the same defense-news snippet is scored by a classical TF-IDF logistic-regression baseline and by the LLM, both against the same 54-row human gold set.">
      <title>Decision fork for the classifier bake-off: the same defense-news snippet is scored by a classical TF-IDF logistic-regression baseline and by the LLM, both against the same 54-row human gold set.</title>
      <defs>
        <marker id="bf-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <!-- Shared input on the left. -->
      <text class="bf-lane" x="4" y="12">INPUT</text>
      <rect class="bf-box" x="4" y="48" width="70" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="bf-title" x="39" y="68" text-anchor="middle">snippet</text>
      <text class="bf-micro" x="39" y="80" text-anchor="middle">one row</text>

      <!-- Split: classical above, LLM below — both face the same gold. -->
      <path class="bf-edge" d="M 74 68 L 90 68 L 90 40 L 106 40"
            marker-end="url(#bf-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="bf-edge" d="M 90 68 L 90 96 L 106 96"
            marker-end="url(#bf-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

      <text class="bf-lane" x="110" y="12">ARMS</text>
      <rect class="bf-box" x="110" y="22" width="78" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="bf-title" x="149" y="38" text-anchor="middle">TF-IDF + LR</text>
      <text class="bf-micro" x="149" y="50" text-anchor="middle">classical</text>

      <rect class="bf-box" x="110" y="78" width="78" height="36" rx="2" />
      <text class="bf-title" x="149" y="94" text-anchor="middle">LLM</text>
      <text class="bf-micro" x="149" y="106" text-anchor="middle">forced tool</text>

      <!-- Merge into shared gold. -->
      <path class="bf-edge" d="M 188 40 L 204 40 L 204 68 L 218 68"
            marker-end="url(#bf-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />
      <path class="bf-edge" d="M 188 96 L 204 96 L 204 68" aria-hidden="true" />

      <text class="bf-lane" x="200" y="12">GOLD</text>
      <rect class="bf-gold" x="222" y="48" width="34" height="40" rx="2" />
      <text class="bf-gold-text" x="239" y="68" text-anchor="middle">n=54</text>
      <text class="bf-micro" x="239" y="80" text-anchor="middle">human</text>

      <text class="bf-micro" x="130" y="140" text-anchor="middle">same rows both arms &middot; paired McNemar</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="bake-fork-caption">
      One snippet, two arms, one human gold set. The table below is the score;
      the fork is why the comparison is fair.
    </p>

    <table class="metrics" id="bakeoff">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Against human gold, n=54</th><th>Classical baseline</th><th>The LLM</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Category accuracy</td><td>72.2%</td><td>92.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Domain accuracy</td><td>66.7%</td><td>92.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cost per article</td><td>$0.00</td><td>one API call</td></tr>
        <tr><td>All 54 rows, wall-clock</td><td>4.8&nbsp;ms</td><td>network-bound, seconds</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The LLM wins by 20 points on category and 26 on domain. Both arms ran on
      identical rows, so the gap can be tested: a paired exact McNemar returns
      <strong>p=0.013</strong> on category and <strong>p=0.0005</strong> on
      domain. Counted as disagreements, the baseline alone was wrong 14 times to
      the LLM&rsquo;s 3 on category, and 15 to 1 on domain
      (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/decisions/017-classical-baseline-bakeoff.md">ADR-017</a>,
      full report in
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/evals/baseline_eval.txt">baseline_eval.txt</a>).
    </p>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Where the baseline broke</p>
      <p>The misses are not spread evenly. The baseline collapses toward
        whichever class is lexically loud: five of six <code>policy</code>
        stories came back as <code>operations</code>, and six of eleven
        <code>land</code> rows came back as <code>air</code>. Those are the
        labels that depend on what <em>kind of claim</em> an article is making,
        not which words it contains. A treaty and a deployment share a
        vocabulary; only one of them is policy.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What that means for the spend:</strong> the
        LLM is buying the boundaries that need reading comprehension.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Two handicaps</p>
      <p>The training labels came from the Opus judge, not from people, so the
        baseline was learning to imitate a grader that itself disagrees with
        humans about 5&ndash;6% of the time, and then it was tested against those
        humans. Separately, <code>industry</code> had exactly
        <strong>one</strong> training row, which makes that label structurally
        unlearnable. The baseline scored 0.000 on it, as it had to.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why they stay:</strong> both cut against the
        baseline. The same class of unfairness inflated the BM25 lift until the
        baseline was rebuilt. A margin this size survives the caveats.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="try-it">Run the loser</h2>
    <p>
      This is the same fit, running in your tab: the model exported as data,
      about 208&nbsp;KB over the wire, no server and no key. It is the arm that
      <a href="#bakeoff">lost the bake-off</a>. Watching the wrong class light
      up is faster than reading the confusion matrix.
    </p>

    <div class="wide bdemo" id="baseline-demo">
      <p class="bdemo-kicker">A row from the gold set, or your own sentence</p>
      <div class="bdemo-chips" id="bdemo-chips" role="group" aria-label="Example rows from the human gold set"></div>

      <label class="bdemo-label" for="bdemo-input">Text to classify</label>
      <textarea id="bdemo-input" class="bdemo-input" rows="3" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="Paste a defense-news sentence&hellip;"></textarea>
      <p class="bdemo-status" id="bdemo-status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Pick an example, or type a sentence.</p>

      <div class="bdemo-cols">
        <section class="bdemo-panel" aria-labelledby="bdemo-live-h">
          <h3 class="bdemo-h" id="bdemo-live-h">Baseline, live in this tab</h3>
          <div class="bdemo-axis">
            <p class="bdemo-axis-name">category</p>
            <p class="bdemo-verdict" id="bdemo-verdict-category">&mdash;</p>
            <div class="bdemo-bars" id="bdemo-bars-category" role="img" aria-label="Per-class probabilities for category"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bdemo-axis">
            <p class="bdemo-axis-name">operational_domain</p>
            <p class="bdemo-verdict" id="bdemo-verdict-operational_domain">&mdash;</p>
            <div class="bdemo-bars" id="bdemo-bars-operational_domain" role="img" aria-label="Per-class probabilities for operational domain"></div>
          </div>
          <p class="bdemo-missing"><strong>region: not covered.</strong> The
            shipped classifier assigns a third axis. The baseline never had
            region labels to train on, so there is nothing to draw here.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="bdemo-panel" aria-labelledby="bdemo-ref-h">
          <h3 class="bdemo-h" id="bdemo-ref-h">Recorded, not run</h3>
          <div id="bdemo-reference"></div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <p class="bdemo-prov">The model is vendored from
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/web/README.md">the
        classifier repo</a> at commit <code>7b61315</code> &mdash; the export
        and its inference module travel as a pair, and the repo&rsquo;s parity
        gate asserts this JavaScript reproduces scikit-learn&rsquo;s own
        decision function to 1e-6. The gold and LLM columns are read out of
        committed artifacts
        (<a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/data/gold/gold.csv"><code>data/gold/gold.csv</code></a>,
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/defense-news-classifier/blob/main/evals/gold_predictions_v3.csv"><code>evals/gold_predictions_v3.csv</code></a>).
        No API is called from this page.</p>

      <noscript><p class="bdemo-prov">This demo needs JavaScript. Without it the
        numbers above the fold still stand on their own.</p></noscript>
    </div>

  </main>


  <script is:inline src="../assets/theme.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="../assets/last-updated.js"></script>
  <script is:inline type="module" src="baseline-demo.js"></script>
</Base>
